n  a second chance  he lived out that second chance often in  loungewear at his florida mansion just  west of miami beach  when he died of a heart attack at only  48  his wife was so grief-stricken she  covered almost everything in the home  with bed sheets  it was full of old pictures fancy china  furniture whatever bling was called back  in the 1930s al capone  sure had a lot of it  this is uh just a regular tie clip that  well it's not just a regular  that's a pretty  [Laughter]  nearly 200 items including the more  provocative ones you might expect of a  gangster go on the auction block this  week  this gun was kind of his  protection  and i think it saved his life on a  number of occasions and so he called it  his sweetheart and he carried this with  him everywhere as far as i know yes  capone's semi-automatic colt is expected  to go for hundreds of thousands of  dollars it's  that part of his life the mob boss part  that intrigues people still  how do you square his two lives his  public persona as  a gangster  and his private persona as a grandfather  you know i can't  clearly he was capable of  compartmentalizing his life do you think  he was capable of murdering people  i i have no idea  that's not the capone she knew  this is  to my dear son  well son of my heart  here is dear father who loves you one of  the most personal items in the auction  is this handwritten letter capone wrote  to his son lyan's father while still  imprisoned in alcatraz  until next week give mother a nice big  kiss for me  and god bless you both  your dear father alfonse capone  and that is the person my grandmother  always knew he was she never gave up  hope no matter how heartfelt it may  sound  for some  the auction  is almost heartbreaking it's morally  sort of murky isn't it it is and i just  find it as very offensive to the crime  victims nina cerlano besselman is a  former california prosecutor she has no  connection to chicago or to capone but  she does have a connection to